**`ttlCache.ts`** is an in-memory cache with TTL expiry, LRU eviction and de-duplication of concurrent loads:

```ts
type Entry<V> = { value: V; expiresAt: number };

export class TtlCache<V> {
  private entries = new Map<string, Entry<V>>();
  private inFlight = new Map<string, Promise<V>>();
  private ttlMs: number;
  private maxEntries: number;

  constructor(ttlMs: number, maxEntries = 10_000) {
    this.ttlMs = ttlMs;
    this.maxEntries = maxEntries;
  }

  async getOrLoad(key: string, load: () => Promise<V>): Promise<V> {
    const hit = this.entries.get(key);
    if (hit && hit.expiresAt > Date.now()) {
      // Re-insert so Map order tracks recency (LRU).
      this.entries.delete(key);
      this.entries.set(key, hit);
      return hit.value;
    }

    // Concurrent misses for the same key share one upstream call.
    const pending = this.inFlight.get(key);
    if (pending) return pending;

    const promise = load()
      .then((value) => {
        // Skip the write if the key was invalidated while we were loading.
        if (this.inFlight.get(key) === promise) this.set(key, value);
        return value;
      })
      .finally(() => {
        if (this.inFlight.get(key) === promise) this.inFlight.delete(key);
      });
    this.inFlight.set(key, promise);
    return promise;
  }

  invalidate(key: string): void {
    this.entries.delete(key);
    this.inFlight.delete(key);
  }

  private set(key: string, value: V): void {
    this.entries.delete(key);
    this.entries.set(key, { value, expiresAt: Date.now() + this.ttlMs });
    if (this.entries.size > this.maxEntries) {
      this.entries.delete(this.entries.keys().next().value!);
    }
  }
}
```

**`userProfileService.ts`** wraps the profile read and invalidates on write; `fetchUserProfile` and `saveUserProfile` in `./userProfileApi` are placeholders for your existing calls:

```ts
import { TtlCache } from "./ttlCache";
import { fetchUserProfile, saveUserProfile, type UserProfile } from "./userProfileApi";

const profileCache = new TtlCache<UserProfile>(60_000);

export function getUserProfile(userId: string): Promise<UserProfile> {
  return profileCache.getOrLoad(userId, () => fetchUserProfile(userId));
}

export async function updateUserProfile(
  userId: string,
  patch: Partial<UserProfile>,
): Promise<UserProfile> {
  const updated = await saveUserProfile(userId, patch);
  profileCache.invalidate(userId);
  return updated;
}
```

Failed loads are not cached, so an upstream error is retried on the next request.

Three things to check before using it:

- **Viewer-dependent responses:** the cache is keyed by `userId` only. If the profile response differs by who is asking (private fields, permissions), the key must include the viewer or you will leak one viewer's view to another.
- **Multiple instances:** the cache is per process, so an update on one server leaves other servers stale for up to the TTL. If you run more than one instance, back it with Redis instead.
- **TTL:** 60 seconds and 10,000 entries are placeholders I picked, not values derived from your traffic.

